Experiment - 11

Title : Detecting and Mitigating Phishing Attacks using Gophish (Authorised Phishing Simulation)

Aim : To set up a safe, authorised phishing simulation using Gophish, analyse how phishing emails are built and spotted, and measure awareness.

Tools / Requirements :
- Kali Linux / Windows OS
- Gophish Framework
- Test SMTP Mail Server Setup
- Web Browser & Consenting Test Users

Theory :
Phishing is a deceptive technique where attackers send fake emails to trick users into revealing sensitive data. Specialized forms include Spear phishing (targeting individuals), Whaling (aiming at high-level executives), Smishing (via SMS), and Vishing (voice call phishing). To safely simulate these attacks, Gophish is used—an open-source defensive and training framework that runs on Kali or Windows, launching a web dashboard on port 3333 to track open, click, and mock data submissions.

Procedure (Steps Performed) :
1. Download and extract the Gophish zip archive.
2. Make the Gophish binary executable (chmod +x gophish) and run it with sudo ./gophish in terminal.
3. Retrieve the generated admin password from the console and log into the Gophish dashboard at https://127.0.0.1:3333.
4. Create a Sending Profile using a test SMTP server (e.g., Lab Sender).
5. Draft an Email Template (the lure message) using the {{.URL}} and {{.FirstName}} placeholders.
6. Build a Landing Page that safely registers clicks while displaying educational warnings.
7. Create a Users & Groups list containing the emails of consenting test participants.
8. Launch the Campaign, tying the profile, template, page, and group together to send and track the emails.

Gophish Five Building Blocks :
- Sending Profile: SMTP details.
- Email Template: Lure messages.
- Landing Page: Educational fake site.
- Users & Groups: Consenting test list.
- Campaign: Integrates all blocks.

